<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mysore</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Mysuru</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mysore (Mysuru) is a city in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. According to tradition it became the seat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wadiyar-dynasty">Wodeyar dynasty</a> in 1399, and the Wodeyars rose from local chiefs under Vijayanagara to rulers of the Kingdom of Mysore. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tipu-sultan">Tipu Sultan</a>'s fall in 1799 it was again the capital of their princely state. It is known for its palace and its Dasara festival.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mysore.jpg" alt="Mysore" width="320" height="561" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sathyaprakash01 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mysuru_Montage.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Mysore stands at the foot of the Chamundi Hills at an altitude of 770 metres, about 140 kilometres south-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengaluru">Bangalore</a>, between the Kaveri River to the north and its tributary the Kabini to the south. The name is an anglicised form of Mahiṣūru, the abode of Mahiṣa. Mahiṣa is Mahishasura, a mythical demon who, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> belief, ruled the area and was killed by the goddess Chamundeshwari, whose temple stands on Chamundi Hill. The city was officially renamed Mysuru on 1 November 2014.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>At the start of the 16th century a village named Puragere occupied the site where Mysore Palace now stands. Chamaraja Wodeyar III (1513–1553) built the Mahishūru Fort in 1524. The Kingdom of Mysore, governed by the Wodeyar family, was at first a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vijayanagara-empire">Vijayanagara Empire</a>. After that empire declined following the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-talikota">Battle of Talikota</a> in 1565, Mysore gradually became independent, and by the time of King Narasaraja Wodeyar in 1637 it was a sovereign state. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srirangapatna">Seringapatam</a>, the present-day Srirangapatna, was its capital from 1610. In the 17th century, under Narasaraja Wodeyar I and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chikka-devaraja-wodeyar">Chikka Devaraja Wodeyar</a>, the kingdom annexed large areas of what is now southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a> and parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamil-nadu">Tamil Nadu</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyder-ali">Hyder Ali</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tipu-sultan">Tipu Sultan</a>, who held power briefly in succession, promoted the planting of mulberry trees and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> in the region and fought four wars against the British. Most of the city's modern development is attributed to the Wodeyar maharajas, patrons of art and culture. A municipality was established in 1888, and a railway from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengaluru">Bangalore</a> opened in 1882. The first free English school was founded in 1833, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharaja">Maharaja</a>'s College in 1864 and the Mysore Sanskrit Pāthaśhāla in 1876. The Mysore economic conference of 1911 planned industrial growth, which led to the Mysore Sandalwood Oil Factory in 1917 and the Sri Krishnarajendra Mills in 1920.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Mysore is the administrative headquarters of Mysore district and is known as the City of Palaces, with Mysore Palace, also called the Amba Vilas Palace, at the centre of its Dasara festival. King Raja Wodeyar I introduced the ten days of Dasara in 1610. On the tenth day, Vijayadashami, the idol of Chamundeshwari is carried on an elephant in procession from the palace to Bannimantapa. Mysore also gives its name to Mysore painting, the sweet Mysore pak and Mysore silk saris.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mysore” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Anglo-<b>Mysore</b> wars (1766–1799) and the Anglo-Maratha wars (1772–1818) left the Company in control of much of India south of the Sutlej, and after the Marathas' defeat no native power threatened it.</blockquote><a href="/empire/company-rule-in-india">Company rule in India · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Kingdom of <b>Mysore</b> grew from a small chiefdom in the southern Karnataka plateau.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-mysore-1399-1799">Kingdom of Mysore (1399-1799) · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Under Dharma Raja, who refused to surrender Hindu refugees from <b>Mysore</b>'s occupation of Malabar, Tipu Sultan's army entered Cochin from Coimbatore in November 1789 and on 28 December attacked the Nedunkotta and was defeated at the Battle of Nedumkotta.</blockquote><a href="/empire/travancore">Travancore · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>British and allied troops breached the walls of <b>Mysore</b>'s capital, and Tipu Sultan died fighting at a gate.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-4">4 May in history</a></li></ul></section>
